KUF port (M9): --vulkan_texture_load_direct_to_image, load render-to-texture results straight into the image

A texture made from a resolution-scaled resolve is loaded every time the game renders it again: a compute shader
untiles the scaled resolve data into a staging buffer, and vkCmdCopyBufferToImage copies that into the image.
With the option, resolution-scaled textures of one 2D slice and one mip level whose host format the load writes bit
for bit (RGBA16 formats: 64 bpb; RGBA8, RGB10A2, RG16, R32 formats: 32 bpb and depth float) are created with
storage usage and an R16G16B16A16_UINT or R32_UINT view (listed with VkImageFormatListCreateInfo), and are loaded
by variants of the 64 bpb, 32 bpb and depth float scaled load shaders that write the same bits into the image
(texture_load_*_scaled_image_cs.h, made by the KUF port's tools/spirv_load_to_image.py from the shipped shaders:
every instruction reading and converting the data is kept, the two stores into the staging buffer become image
writes, a texel past the level's width is not written; only core storage image formats). The texture takes a new
usage, kComputeWrite (GENERAL layout), and is transitioned from it like any other usage when sampled. Each such
texture gets its storage view and a descriptor set (a pool of 1024, freed with the texture) on its first direct
load; if none is left the load takes the staging buffer as before. Default off. Needs vkFreeDescriptorSets in the
device function table (added by patch 0012).

A texture is loaded directly only when the device supports storage images (optimal tiling) for both its format
and the view format (vkGetPhysicalDeviceFormatProperties at initialization; M11, Steam Deck: an image created
with a usage its format does not support is invalid); the others take the staging buffer. The log names them.

